ID: sec:doc:clinical_policy:12B:6.2.1
Title: 6.2.1 Certification Requirements
Section: 6.2.1
Policy: 12B
Type: clinical_policy
Effective: 2020-01-15
Citation link: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

Content:
Provider agency types may include home health agencies, home care agencies, hospices, health departments, hospitals, departments of social services, federally qualified health clinics, rural health clinics, community-based organizations (CBO), and local management entities.
Agencies shall obtain certification from NC Medicaid, or DHHS designated contractor, to be considered for enrollment with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider.
To qualify for reimbursement for HIV Case Management services, a provider shall meet all the criteria below:
Have a documented successful record of three (3) years of providing or managing HIV Case Management programs;
Ensure the provision of HIV Case Management services by qualified CM's as described in Subsections 6.1.1, 6.1.2, and 6.1.3 of this policy;
Ensure supervision of HIV CM by qualified supervisors as described in Subsections 6.1.4 and 6.1.5 of this policy;
Enroll each physical site with NC Medicaid as a provider of HIV Case Management services in accordance with §1902(a)(23) of the Social Security Act;
Meet applicable state and federal laws governing the participation of providers in the Medicaid program;
Maintain certification as a qualified provider of HIV Case Management services;
Demonstrate compliance with initial and ongoing certification processes;
Demonstrate compliance with the monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan;
Allow NC Medicaid, or DHHS designated contractor, to review beneficiary records and inspect agency operation and financial records;
Notify NC Medicaid, or DHHS designated contractor, of proposed changes, such as business owner or name change, address or telephone number change, or plans for business dissolution within 30 calendar days of proposed change, and no later than five business days of the actual change; and
Within one year of enrollment with Medicaid as a provider, the provider must have achieved national accreditation with at least one of the designated accrediting agencies. Designated accrediting agencies are the following: Accreditation Commission for Health Care (ACHC), Commission on Accreditation of Rehabilitation Facilities (CARF), Community Health Accreditation Partner (CHAP), Council on Accreditation (COA), Public Health Accreditation Board (PHAB), The Joint Commission, and Utilization Review Accreditation Commission (URAC).

